---
/**
 * One box over the districts, the corpus and the sources.
 *
 * A route rather than only a header control, so a search can be linked and so the header's box has
 * somewhere to submit to when scripting is off. With scripting on it filters as you type.
 */
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { loadCorpus } from "../lib/corpus.ts";
import { loadFeed } from "../lib/feed.ts";
import { count } from "../lib/format.ts";
import * as routes from "../lib/routes.ts";

const { bundle } = loadFeed();
const corpus = loadCorpus();
---

<Base
  title="Search"
  description="Search Ohio's school districts, the funding corpus, and the sources behind it."
  image={routes.og.page("search")}
  imageAlt="Search Ohio's school districts and the funding corpus"
  section="districts"
>
  <h1>Search</h1>
  <p class="sub">
    {count(bundle.statewide.districts)} districts, {corpus.nodes.length} corpus nodes and {
      corpus.sources.length
    } sources. Type a district name, an IRN, or a concept.
  </p>

  <div class="card">
    <form class="filters" id="search-form" role="search">
      <div class="field">
        <label for="s-q">Search</label>
        <input id="s-q" name="q" type="search" autocomplete="off" placeholder="Cleveland, 043786, guarantee, H.B. 920…" />
      </div>
      <p class="count" id="s-count" aria-live="polite"></p>
    </form>
  </div>

  <div id="search-out"></div>

  <noscript>
    <div class="card">
      <p class="note">
        Searching needs JavaScript. Without it, the lists are still browsable in full:
        <a href="/districts">every district</a>, <a href="/wiki">the corpus by class</a>, and
        <a href="/wiki/source">every source</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </noscript>

  <script>
    import "../scripts/search.ts";
  </script>
</Base>
